<!-- STAGE 1 -->
<section class="stage">
  <div class="stage-head"><div class="num">1</div><h2>Design the action contract before you build anything</h2></div>
  <p class="goal">Writing, not clicking. Most agent failures are traced back to this page.</p>
  <div class="stage-body">
    <p>Decide what the agent may do, and write each operation as a precise contract. Two operations are enough:</p>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Operation</th><th>Reads or writes</th><th>Needs confirmation</th></tr>
      <tr><td><code>get_leave_balance</code></td><td>Reads a balance for the caller</td><td>No</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>file_leave_request</code></td><td>Writes a request record</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>Then write the function schema. Every description is read by the model at decision time:</p>
<pre><code>{
  "functions": [
    {
      "name": "get_leave_balance",
      "description": "Return the remaining paid leave days for the current employee in the current leave year. Use for questions about how much leave someone has left. Do not use to file or cancel leave.",
      "parameters": {
        "leave_type": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "One of: annual, sick, casual. Ask the employee if not stated.",
          "required": true
        }
      },
      "requireConfirmation": "DISABLED"
    },
    {
      "name": "file_leave_request",
      "description": "Submit a new leave request for approval. Only use after the employee has stated the leave type, the start date and the end date, and has confirmed the dates.",
      "parameters": {
        "leave_type": {"type": "string", "description": "annual, sick or casual", "required": true},
        "start_date": {"type": "string", "description": "ISO date, YYYY-MM-DD", "required": true},
        "end_date":   {"type": "string", "description": "ISO date, YYYY-MM-DD, on or after start_date", "required": true}
      },
      "requireConfirmation": "ENABLED"
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>

    <p>Notice what each description does: it says when to use the operation <em>and</em> when not to, names the exact accepted values, and states the preconditions. Vague or overlapping descriptions are the direct cause of an agent calling the wrong operation.</p>

    <div class="callout lens">
      <span class="tag">Exam lens</span>
      <p>"The agent invokes the wrong action group" is a stock troubleshooting question, and the answer is nearly always schema clarity — descriptions, parameter names, disambiguation between similar operations — rather than a bigger model or a longer instruction block. Also know user confirmation as the built-in control for irreversible actions.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="done">
      <span class="tag">Done when</span>
      <label><input type="checkbox">Both operations have descriptions stating when to use and when not to use them.</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox">The write operation requires confirmation and the read operation does not.</label>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- STAGE 2 -->
<section class="stage">
  <div class="stage-head"><div class="num">2</div><h2>Build the action Lambda and lock its front door</h2></div>
  <p class="goal">Console for creation, code for the handler, CLI for the resource policy.</p>
  <div class="stage-body">
    <p>Create a DynamoDB table <code>leave-records</code> with partition key <code>employee_id</code> and sort key <code>record_id</code>. Then a Lambda named <code>leave-actions</code>, Python 3.13, 30-second timeout, with read and write permissions on that table only.</p>

<pre><code>import json, os, uuid, datetime, boto3

TABLE = boto3.resource("dynamodb").Table(os.environ["TABLE_NAME"])
ALLOWED_TYPES = {"annual", "sick", "casual"}

def lambda_handler(event, context):
    fn     = event["function"]
    params = {p["name"]: p["value"] for p in event.get("parameters", [])}
    # Identity comes from the invoking application, never from the model.
    employee_id = (event.get("sessionAttributes") or {}).get("employee_id")

    if not employee_id:
        return _reply(event, {"error": "no employee identity on session"})

    if fn == "get_leave_balance":
        result = _balance(employee_id, params.get("leave_type"))
    elif fn == "file_leave_request":
        result = _file(employee_id, params)
    else:
        result = {"error": f"unknown function {fn}"}

    return _reply(event, result)

def _balance(employee_id, leave_type):
    if leave_type not in ALLOWED_TYPES:
        return {"error": f"leave_type must be one of {sorted(ALLOWED_TYPES)}"}
    item = TABLE.get_item(
        Key={"employee_id": employee_id, "record_id": f"balance#{leave_type}"}
    ).get("Item")
    return {"leave_type": leave_type, "days_remaining": int(item["days"]) if item else 0}

def _file(employee_id, p):
    lt, start, end = p.get("leave_type"), p.get("start_date"), p.get("end_date")
    if lt not in ALLOWED_TYPES:
        return {"error": f"leave_type must be one of {sorted(ALLOWED_TYPES)}"}
    try:
        s = datetime.date.fromisoformat(start)
        e = datetime.date.fromisoformat(end)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return {"error": "start_date and end_date must be YYYY-MM-DD"}
    if e &lt; s:
        return {"error": "end_date must be on or after start_date"}
    if (e - s).days &gt; 30:
        return {"error": "requests longer than 30 days need HR approval offline"}

    record_id = f"request#{uuid.uuid4()}"
    TABLE.put_item(Item={
        "employee_id": employee_id, "record_id": record_id,
        "leave_type": lt, "start_date": start, "end_date": end,
        "status": "submitted",
    })
    return {"request_id": record_id, "status": "submitted",
            "days": (e - s).days + 1}

def _reply(event, body):
    return {"messageVersion": "1.0", "response": {
        "actionGroup": event["actionGroup"],
        "function": event["function"],
        "functionResponse": {"responseBody": {"TEXT": {"body": json.dumps(body)}}},
    }}</code></pre>

    <p>Two design points. Every argument is validated in the Lambda, because arguments arrive from a language model and are untrusted input. And the employee identity comes from session attributes set by the calling application, never from a parameter the model can fill in — otherwise the agent can be talked into reading someone else's balance.</p>

    <p>Now the resource policy. Bedrock invokes this function, so grant it explicitly and narrowly:</p>
<pre><code>aws lambda add-permission \
  --function-name leave-actions \
  --statement-id bedrock-agent-invoke \
  --action lambda:InvokeFunction \
  --principal bedrock.amazonaws.com \
  --source-account &lt;ACCOUNT_ID&gt; \
  --source-arn "arn:aws:bedrock:ap-south-1:&lt;ACCOUNT_ID&gt;:agent/&lt;AGENT_ID&gt;" \
  --region ap-south-1</code></pre>

    <p>You will not have the agent ID until stage 3, so create the agent first and come back, or use a placeholder ARN and tighten it immediately after. The same confused-deputy conditions from Lab 2 stage 4 appear here — <code>--source-arn</code> is what stops any other Bedrock agent, in any account, from invoking your function.</p>

    <div class="callout lens">
      <span class="tag">Exam lens</span>
      <p>Two permission layers per action group: the <em>agent's</em> service role must allow <code>lambda:InvokeFunction</code>, and the <em>function's</em> resource policy must allow the Bedrock principal scoped by source ARN. Questions describing an agent that reasons correctly but fails at execution point at whichever layer the scenario omitted.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="done">
      <span class="tag">Done when</span>
      <label><input type="checkbox">A direct Lambda test with a simulated agent event returns a valid <code>functionResponse</code>.</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox">Invalid dates and an unknown leave type both return a clear error rather than throwing.</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox">The resource policy names your agent ARN, not a wildcard.</label>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- STAGE 3 -->
<section class="stage">
  <div class="stage-head"><div class="num">3</div><h2>Create the agent and attach both capabilities</h2></div>
  <p class="goal">Console. Instructions, action group, knowledge base, service role.</p>
  <div class="stage-body">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Bedrock → Agents → Create agent</strong>, name it <code>leave-assistant</code>. Let the console create the service role.</li>
      <li>Select a model that supports agents and tool use in your region.</li>
      <li>Write the instructions. Aim for a role, a scope, and explicit rules:</li>
    </ol>

<pre><code>You are the leave assistant for SourceFuse employees.

You can answer questions about leave policy using the knowledge base, check
the employee's leave balance, and file leave requests.

Rules:
- Answer policy questions only from the knowledge base. If the knowledge base
  does not cover it, say so and suggest contacting HR. Never guess a policy.
- Before filing a request, confirm the leave type, start date and end date
  back to the employee and wait for agreement.
- Check the balance before filing an annual leave request. If the balance is
  insufficient, say so and do not file.
- Never discuss another employee's leave. You only have access to the person
  you are speaking with.
- Do not give legal, tax or medical advice.</code></pre>

    <ol start="4">
      <li>Add an action group named <code>leave-operations</code>: <em>Define with function details</em>, select the <code>leave-actions</code> Lambda, and paste the two functions from stage 1.</li>
      <li>Add the Lab 2 knowledge base, with an instruction telling the agent what is in it: "Company leave, travel and expense policy documents. Search here for any question about entitlements, eligibility or process."</li>
      <li>Save, then <strong>Prepare</strong>. Nothing you changed takes effect until you do.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Check the agent's service role in IAM. It should hold <code>bedrock:InvokeModel</code> on the model, <code>bedrock:Retrieve</code> on the knowledge base, and <code>lambda:InvokeFunction</code> on your function — and its trust policy should carry the same <code>aws:SourceAccount</code> and <code>aws:SourceArn</code> conditions you read in Lab 2.</p>

    <div class="callout verify">
      <span class="tag">Verify</span>
      <p>Console-created agent roles have historically required a name beginning with a specific prefix, and hand-made roles that ignore this have failed in confusing ways. If you write the role yourself, check the current naming requirement in the Bedrock user guide rather than assuming.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="done">
      <span class="tag">Done when</span>
      <label><input type="checkbox">The agent is prepared with one action group and one knowledge base attached.</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox">In the test panel, a policy question is answered from the knowledge base with sources.</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox">In the test panel, a balance question triggers the action group.</label>
    </div>
